we've been following what I have  generally characterized as a definitive  and decided war on Free Press by the  Trump administration and this has  multiple components and none of these  should come as a surprise to you because  I've been talking about these over time  the idea of the media as the enemy of  the American people that having trouble  today I can't I can't imagine why  journalism licenses being required to  practice media libel lawsuits made  easier we had of course countless tweets  from Donald Trump about this including  the following tweet the fake news media  failing New York Times NBC News ABC CBS  CNN is not my enemy it is the enemy of  the American people and last week during  a Jim Acosta and Sarah Huckabee Sanders  it's being described as an exchange I  call it more of a kerfuffle I think  maybe high rated a tirade of sorts the  White House refused to deny that the  press was the enemy of the American  people and in fact I would argue Sarah  Huckabee Sanders sort of confirmed it by  saying Trump has already told us what  his position is on that and I speak on  the behalf of the President and we now  have a new poll to sort of add to our  fleshing out of whether this is a  strategy that's working 43 percent not  4.3 percent not 14.3%  not 24.3% 43 percent nearly half of  Republicans believe that Donald Trump  should be able to unilaterally just say  this media outlet it's over whatever the  year fired equivalent is that you would  say to a media outlet when they're going  away 48% of Republicans in the same poll  agree that the media is the enemy of the  American people and this is where you  have to understand the I don't want to  call it brilliance but the power of  these campaigns by Trump where even  without saying straight up all media is  the enemy of the American people or  media in principle are the enemies of  the American people half of Republicans  are believing it and dump Trump didn't  even have to say it in as explicit terms  as he could have now in comparison 12%  of Democrats and 21% of independents  agree  about president Trump closing down media  outlets those numbers are still higher  than I would like to see right like the  number should be if anyone who  understands America is asked should the  president be able to say you are off the  air the answer should always be no we  have systems in place we have limits as  to what can legally be reported if your  FCC bound because of your broadcast  platforms there are rules governing the  answer should never be yes the president  should get to say you're off the air  whether it's 12% for Dems 21 for  independents or 43 for Republicans it's  all horrible yeah I'd like to see it at  0% of course we'll never get that maybe  just a few percentage points I would  take because this is the American public  after all very well you it's hard to get  zero percent of Americans agreeing on  anything right let me go to a different  way it's hard to get a hundred percent  of Americans agreeing on anything  overall 57 percent of respondents  believe that media is necessary to keep  the Trump administration honest I would  agree with that  13% of Americans agreed that President  Trump should close down mainstream news  outlets like CNN The Washington Post in  the New York Times what I go to is  number one there's a partisan element  right like if a Democrat were president  right now you would probably see  Democrats to a slightly higher degree  saying Obama should get to close down  whatever and Republicans lower but it's  not just hypocrisy there is no question  that the so-called constitutional  conservatives who claim to be all about  the Bill of Rights and separation of  powers and checks and balances and all  this stuff they seemed completely  unconcerned with the fact that there is  nothing in the First Amendment which  would allow for a president to just say  this media outlet doesn't get to  continue or that media outlet doesn't  get to continue and part of what has  been going on that predates the Trump  administration that fuels this is a  distrust of media in general right in  addition to the likely partisan divide  that there is in general you have a high  distrust of media and I often have asked  about this right people will say to me  David  if  you're so often critical of media  outlets how are you now worried about  the fact that Trump is criticizing media  what you have to understand is there's a  difference between identifying a  particular media outlet that's doing bad  reporting and criticizing them that's  one thing versus me saying the president  should get to pick which media outlets  exist these are completely unrelated  problems that we're dealing with and  unfortunately a lot of people are going  in the wrong direction on this  seventy-two percent of respondents  believe it should be easier to sue  reporters willingly publishing false  information remember the key word is  easier there's already laws that prevent  reporters or punish reporters from  punishing Ephrem publishing willingly  false information and it's working and  it's being reflected by Trump's own  followers here's one example where CNN's  reporter Jim Acosta was just yelled at  and badgered during a Trump rally in  Florida recently  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  so there are a number of disturbing  angles to this generally number one the  authoritarian tendencies shutting down  media lack of press freedom right we see  this in places like Venezuela we've seen  it in China North Korea Turkey doesn't  like the internet we heard from  president air21 that he's increasingly  against the internet every day when you  see leaders one thing to have this level  of control that is an authoritarian  tendency and worse when you've got half  of one party saying you want them to  have that control that is really really  scary beyond that I already mentioned  there's the constitutional angle here  what exceptions are there to freedom of  the press that would allow a president  to close down a media outlet but in the  end it's just more hypocrisy than it is  anything else the principles don't  matter  there is a contingent out there and it  is where the Alex Jones story brought a  lot of them out right when it's Alex  Jones being taken off of youtube youtube  is unjustifiably censoring Alex Jones  which isn't the government by the way  but then all of a sudden Republicans are  asked would you want the president  meaning the government to just be able  to select media outlets to shut down and  half of them say yes it doesn't Square  there is no way where both of those  views can be held simultaneously without  huge cognitive dissonance I think we all  see what Trump is doing here I don't  think he would actively pursue shutting  down media outlets and he wouldn't say  anything for that effect but he calls  the media fake news and he builds this  distrust among his supporters so that  they then fill in the blanks and reason  that it's important for us to shut down  these outlets Trump's goal is to not  have to shut down the media outlets to  make them completely uneffective if you  turn public opinion against them enough  if you frame them as the enemies of the  American people and simultaneously  spread so much diss and misinformation  that people don't know what to believe  you won't need to shut down any of the  media outlets because they won't be  functioning as intended to begin with

